            <div class="post-body__intro reveal">
              <p class="post-body__lead">After 15 years and 135+ construction projects across Delaware County and the Main Line, I've learned that every house tells you something the previous owner didn't. It's in the floor that flexes three inches when you walk across it, or the basement wall that sweats in July even after the last contractor "waterproofed" it. This guide is written from the job site — what we actually build, how we sequence multi-trade work, and what really drives the cost and timeline of a construction project in this area.</p>
              <p>Most contractor websites list the same services in the same order. Ours is no different on that page — but this post is different. It's built on project records, not brochure copy. I'm going to show you how two of our recent Delaware County projects actually unfolded, what we found when we opened the walls, and what it cost. Then we'll get into the services themselves.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Two ProBrothers Projects in Delaware County — What Actually Happened</h2>

              <div class="post-workshop">
                <div class="post-workshop__label">From the Workshop: What Daniil Notices</div>
                <p>Before I quote any multi-trade project in Delaware County, I'm looking at three things: foundation condition (older Media homes often have stone foundations with no waterproofing membrane — that changes basement scope completely), electrical panel age (a 100-amp panel from the 1970s needs upgrading if you're finishing a basement or adding a kitchen circuit), and load-bearing wall arrangement (many 1950s–1970s Delaware County colonials have a center bearing wall that limits open-plan kitchen work without an engineered beam). You can't see any of that on Zillow. That's why the on-site consultation is non-negotiable before I give a number.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="post-case-study">
                <div class="post-case-study__label">Project Case Study #1 — Verified from ProBrothers Portfolio</div>
                <h4>BlackHorse House — Full Home Remodel, Media, PA</h4>
                <p><strong>Scope:</strong> Complete interior gut and remodel of a neglected single-family home in Media, PA. Structural: removed 2 load-bearing walls to create an open floor plan connecting the kitchen and living room. Kitchen: doubled in size, new layout, custom cabinets, countertops, and appliances. Basement: converted from unfinished concrete to finished living space with a bedroom and full bathroom. Exterior: side entrance relocated to center-front, new windows added on both flanking sides, new concrete walkway poured, full re-roof, board-and-batten siding added to both gables, backyard porch built.</p>
                <p><strong>Why this project was complex:</strong> Wall removal across a two-story home requires a structural engineer's beam calculation, permit pull, and mid-project inspection before closing the ceiling. The basement conversion required egress compliance under PA UCC — which meant a proper egress window cut into an existing concrete foundation wall. The exterior work ran parallel to the interior on a separate crew track to compress the overall timeline.</p>
                <p><strong>Daniil's note:</strong> "We started with a beat-up house that no one would want to go near, and turned it into an incredible finished product with a simple, modern, and very attractive look. The hardest part was the sequencing — you can't finish basement drywall before the structural inspection upstairs passes. Getting that sequence right saved us three weeks."</p>
                <p><strong>Outcome:</strong> Full transformation from derelict condition to move-in ready. Open-plan kitchen-living room, finished basement suite, modernized exterior.</p>
              </div>

              <div class="post-case-study">
                <div class="post-case-study__label">Project Case Study #2 — ProBrothers Portfolio</div>
                <h4>Two-Tone Kitchen Restoration — Delaware County Area</h4>
                <p><strong>Scope:</strong> Complete kitchen demolition — removal of all cabinets, flooring, and appliances — followed by a full repaint and the design, build, and installation of a custom two-tone cabinet set. The result was a kitchen rebuilt entirely to the homeowner's specification from a blank slate.</p>
                <p><strong>Why this project mattered:</strong> Off-the-shelf kitchen cabinets don't fit most Delaware County homes precisely — older homes have irregular wall runs, soffits, and ceiling heights that stock boxes don't account for. Custom-built cabinets gave this client a perfect fit, maximized storage in a tight footprint, and delivered a finish quality that stock cabinetry can't match.</p>
                <p><strong>Daniil's note:</strong> "The two-tone look requires precise alignment across the full run — a 1/16" gap between upper and lower doors is visible and it matters. Our shop builds tight tolerances into every cabinet box before it leaves for the site."</p>
                <p><strong>Outcome:</strong> Completely renovated kitchen with custom cabinetry, new flooring, and a cohesive two-tone design the client selected from our design consultation.</p>
