            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>ProBrothers Construction Services — What We Build</h2>
              <p>Here's an honest breakdown of each service type: what it actually involves, what drives the cost, and what we've learned after 135+ projects in this area.</p>

              <h3>Custom Home Construction</h3>
              <p>Ground-up custom home construction is the longest and most complex engagement we take on. We work from architectural drawings and manage all trades — foundation, framing, MEP (mechanical, electrical, plumbing), insulation, drywall, finish carpentry, and exterior work. Our value here is the carpentry precision that most general contractors outsource: the custom millwork, built-ins, and trim details that distinguish a truly custom home from a spec build. Timeline: 10–18 months depending on size and site complexity.</p>

              <h3>Home Additions</h3>
              <p>Additions are the most permit-intensive projects we handle. Every addition touching the foundation or roofline requires a full PA UCC permit package — structural drawings stamped by a licensed engineer, zoning compliance review, and multiple inspections. We manage that entire process. The most common mistake homeowners make is starting contractor conversations before checking their township's setback requirements — some Delaware County townships have 15-foot side setbacks that eliminate certain addition configurations entirely. We check this at the consultation, before you've committed to anything.</p>

              <h3>Kitchen Remodeling</h3>
              <p>The kitchen is where our carpentry background shows most clearly. We build custom cabinet boxes in our shop, which means we're not constrained by standard sizes. In a 1960s Delaware County colonial with a 12'4" kitchen run and a 7'9" ceiling, stock cabinets leave awkward gaps. We fill the run precisely and build to the ceiling, maximizing storage and creating a cleaner look. Layout changes (moving the sink, adding an island) involve plumbing rough-in, which we coordinate with licensed plumbing subcontractors.</p>

              <h3>Bathroom Renovations</h3>
              <p>Bathroom scope varies significantly based on whether the plumbing stays in place. Cosmetic renovations (new tile, vanity, fixtures — plumbing untouched) are faster and less expensive. Full gut-and-rebuilds with relocated shower or toilet positions require new rough-in and inspection. We've renovated bathrooms in homes where the original tile was concealing water damage that had compromised the subfloor — which is why we always open a small inspection section of the wall before committing to a final scope. No surprises after demo day.</p>

              <h3>Basement Finishing</h3>
              <p>Delaware County homes built before 1980 frequently have unfinished basements with stone or block foundation walls and no waterproofing membrane. Before we frame and insulate, we assess the foundation honestly — a wet basement that gets finished without addressing the water intrusion will fail within a few years. PA UCC requires at minimum one egress opening for any sleeping area in a finished basement; we handle the window cut and framing as part of the project.</p>

              <h3>Mudroom and Storage Builds</h3>
              <p>Mudroom builds are where custom carpentry earns its keep. An off-the-shelf mudroom bench-and-locker system doesn't fit a 7'6" x 4'8" entry hall with a door that swings 90 degrees and an existing HVAC vent in the floor. We design the built-in for the specific space: bench height, locker depth, coat hook placement, and storage volume all sized to the household.</p>

              <h3>Door and Window Installation</h3>
              <p>Door and window work ranges from like-for-like replacements (one day per unit, usually no permit needed) to structural opening modifications (new header engineering, permit required). Delaware County's older housing stock — particularly pre-1970 homes in Media, Springfield, and Swarthmore — frequently has non-standard rough opening dimensions. We measure before we order and either source custom-sized units or adjust the rough opening as part of the install.</p>

              <h3>Exterior Renovations</h3>
              <p>Exterior scope at ProBrothers goes beyond siding replacement. We've relocated entries, added porches, extended covered areas, and coordinated concrete flatwork with structural exterior changes. The BlackHorse House project (above) is a good example of what full exterior renovation looks like when it's coordinated with a full interior remodel — the exterior and interior crews ran in parallel, which compressed the timeline by 6–8 weeks compared to sequential scheduling.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <div class="post-permit-box">
                <div class="post-permit-box__label">PA UCC Permits — What Requires One in Delaware County</div>
                <p><strong>Always requires a permit:</strong> structural changes (wall removal, additions, foundation work), new electrical circuits or panel upgrades, plumbing rough-in changes, HVAC additions, basement finishing with sleeping areas (egress required), new decks or porches over 30" above grade.</p>
                <p><strong>Usually no permit needed:</strong> like-for-like window/door replacement (same opening, no structural change), interior painting and flooring, cabinet replacement (no plumbing move), cosmetic bathroom updates (no rough-in change).</p>
                <p><strong>Check your township:</strong> Delaware County is covered by the PA UCC, but individual municipalities may have additional requirements. Media Borough, Springfield Township, and Haverford Township all have slightly different permit intake processes. ProBrothers handles permit applications as part of every applicable project — we know each township's process and typical review timelines.</p>
                <p><strong>Timeline impact:</strong> Permit review in Delaware County typically takes 2–4 weeks for standard projects. Media Borough and Springfield Township are typically faster (1–2 weeks); other municipalities 4–8 weeks; complex additions 3–6 weeks regardless. We factor this into the project schedule at the estimate stage so it doesn't delay your start unexpectedly.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>What to Look For in a Delaware County General Contractor</h2>
              <p>The construction industry has a low barrier to entry. Anyone can call themselves a general contractor. Here's what separates contractors who deliver from those who don't:</p>
              <ul class="post-list post-list--checkmark">
                <li><strong>License and insurance, verified:</strong> Ask for the PA HIC license number and verify it. Ask for the COI (certificate of insurance) with your address listed as additional insured during the project.</li>
                <li><strong>Permit history:</strong> A contractor who suggests "doing it without permits to save money" is putting the risk on you. Unpermitted work can block a future sale, void your homeowner's insurance, and require demolition and redo at your expense.</li>
                <li><strong>References from similar scope:</strong> A contractor with a strong record on kitchen remodels isn't automatically qualified for a full home remodel with structural work. Ask for references on jobs similar in scope to yours.</li>
                <li><strong>Written scope of work:</strong> Any contract that says "general renovation" without a line-item scope is a contract that will generate disputes. Get every trade specified before signing.</li>
                <li><strong>Payment schedule tied to milestones:</strong> Never pay more than 10–15% upfront. Progress payments should align with verified completion milestones, not calendar dates.</li>
              </ul>

              <div class="post-workshop">
                <div class="post-workshop__label">From the Workshop: Daniil on Vetting a Contractor</div>
                <p>The single best question to ask any contractor bidding your project is: "Can I speak to a homeowner whose project was similar to mine, who you finished in the last 12 months?" Not someone from 2019. Not a commercial job. Someone in the same situation as you, recently. How a contractor handles that question — and whether they can produce a reference on the spot — tells you everything about their operation.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Why Choose ProBrothers Construction</h2>
              <p>We're not the biggest contractor in Delaware County. We're a focused crew, and we build that way — every project has Daniil on site, not on paper. Here's what that means in practice:</p>
              <ul class="post-list post-list--checkmark">
                <li><strong>Own carpentry crew, not subcontracted.</strong> Our framing, finish carpentry, and custom millwork are done by ProBrothers employees. No handoff to a sub who's never seen the plans.</li>
                <li><strong>15+ years and 135+ completed projects</strong> in Delaware County and the Main Line. We know the housing stock, the township permit offices, and the suppliers with reliable lead times.</li>
                <li><strong>Free on-site consultation with a written scope.</strong> Daniil walks your space, asks the right questions, and gives you a realistic scope and budget range — usually within one business day of the visit.</li>
                <li><strong>Licensed and insured in Pennsylvania.</strong> PA HIC license, general liability, and workers' compensation. COI available at every project consultation.</li>
                <li><strong>Transparent scheduling and communication.</strong> One point of contact (Daniil or a senior ProBrothers project lead) for the entire job.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>What Homeowners Are Building in Delaware County in 2026</h2>
              <p>Based on the projects we're quoting and completing this year, a few themes stand out:</p>
              <h3>Open-Plan Conversions in Post-War Colonials</h3>
              <p>Delaware County has a large stock of 1950s–1970s colonials with compartmentalized floor plans. The most common request we see is opening the kitchen-to-dining or kitchen-to-living wall. These projects require structural analysis every time — the probability of a load-bearing wall is high in this era's construction. We're seeing budgets of $40,000–$80,000 for wall removal with engineered beam, kitchen expansion, and finish work to match.</p>
              <h3>Basement Conversions as Primary ROI Play</h3>
              <p>With Delaware County home prices elevated, finishing an existing basement is consistently the highest-ROI remodel. You're adding livable square footage — a bedroom, a family room, a home office — without touching the building footprint. The Remodeling Magazine Cost vs. Value data for the Philadelphia metro shows basement finishing at roughly 70–75% cost-to-value.</p>
              <h3>Smart Home Infrastructure During Remodels</h3>
              <p>When walls are open, the cost of adding smart home rough-in is minimal compared to doing it as a standalone retrofit. We regularly rough in wiring for automated lighting, EV charging circuits, whole-home audio, and networked thermostats during kitchen and addition projects. The marginal cost is typically $1,500–$4,000 during a remodel; the retrofit cost after the walls close is $8,000–$15,000+.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About ProBrothers Construction Services</h2>
              <div class="post-faq">
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What construction services does ProBrothers offer in Delaware County, PA?</h3>
                  <p>ProBrothers offers custom home construction, home additions, kitchen remodeling, bathroom renovations, basement finishing, mudroom and storage builds, door and window installation, and exterior renovations in Media, PA and throughout Delaware County and the Main Line. Licensed and insured in Pennsylvania with 15+ years and 135+ completed residential projects. Free quote within one business day.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How much does a home addition cost in Delaware County, PA?</h3>
                  <p>A single-room addition typically runs <strong>$80,000–$150,000</strong> depending on size, finish level, and structural complexity. A second-story addition ranges from <strong>$150,000–$350,000+</strong>. A sunroom or screened porch typically falls between <strong>$30,000–$80,000</strong>. These ranges reflect 2026 Delaware County market rates including permits, materials, and labor.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How long does a full home remodel take?</h3>
                  <p>A focused kitchen or bathroom remodel typically takes <strong>8–14 weeks</strong>. A multi-room or structural project (removing walls, second story, or basement conversion) takes <strong>16–30 weeks</strong>. Structural work requiring engineering permits typically adds 2–4 weeks to the planning phase in most Delaware County townships.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Does ProBrothers handle permits for construction work in Pennsylvania?</h3>
                  <p>Yes. ProBrothers handles all permit applications, inspections, and certificate of occupancy under Pennsylvania's <a href="https://www.dli.pa.gov/Individuals/Construction-Codes-Housing/UCC/Pages/default.aspx" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Uniform Construction Code (PA UCC)</a>. We pull the permits, schedule inspections, and deliver a fully code-compliant job. Permit handling is included in our project scope — there's no separate fee to coordinate this.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Can ProBrothers integrate smart home technology during a remodel?</h3>
                  <p>Yes. When walls are open during a renovation or addition, it's the ideal time to rough in smart home infrastructure — automated lighting, EV charging, whole-home audio, networked thermostat systems. We coordinate with specialty electricians and integrate the rough-in into the construction schedule at no additional mobilization cost.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What makes ProBrothers different from other general contractors in Delaware County?</h3>
                  <p>Most general contractors subcontract their carpentry. We build with our own crew. Daniil Akulov, our founder and master carpenter, is on every site. The person who quoted your project is the same person overseeing the work — you don't lose continuity to a sub who shows up on demo day without context.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What is the first step to getting a construction project started with ProBrothers?</h3>
                  <p>Call or email for a free on-site consultation. Daniil walks the space, assesses the scope, and gives you a realistic budget range — typically within one business day of the visit. Phone: <a href="tel:4847454097">(484) 745-4097</a>. Email: <a href="mailto:info@probrothers.com">info@probrothers.com</a>.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How much does kitchen remodeling cost in Delaware County?</h3>
                  <p>A mid-range kitchen remodel in Delaware County typically runs <strong>$35,000–$70,000</strong> (custom cabinets, quartz countertops, new appliances, tile backsplash, updated lighting). A full gut-and-rebuild with layout changes typically runs <strong>$70,000–$120,000+</strong>. Budget finishes with stock cabinets can start around <strong>$20,000–$30,000</strong>.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Can ProBrothers convert an unfinished basement into livable space?</h3>
                  <p>Yes — basement finishing is one of our most requested services in Delaware County. A typical conversion (framing, insulation, drywall, flooring, lighting, one egress window) runs <strong>$40,000–$80,000</strong>. Adding a full bathroom increases the range to <strong>$55,000–$100,000+</strong>. All basement finishing projects require PA UCC egress compliance and electrical permits, which we handle.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Is ProBrothers licensed and insured in Pennsylvania?</h3>
                  <p>Yes. ProBrothers is fully licensed and insured in Pennsylvania for residential construction and remodeling. PA Home Improvement Contractor (HIC) license on file. General liability and workers' compensation coverage current. Copies available on request at your consultation.</p>
                </div>
